          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong structural set theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            which describes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structuralism"} structural
            mathematics, and
            %em only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structuralism"} structural
            mathematics: Sets are conceived as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            that have
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"}> Set
            ”) which they form (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere65"}> Lawvere 65
            ). This is what essentially all the application of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            in the practice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            actually uses – a point amplified by the approach of the introductory textbook
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#LawvereRosebrugh03"} Lawvere-Rosebrugh 03
          %p
            This is in contrast to traditional
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            (cf
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+versus+structural"} material versus structural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            of sets “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =
            ” alone, and where sets can be elements of other sets, hence where there are sequences of sets which are elements of the next set in the sequence. This latter feature, however, is almost never actually used or even considered in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            itself.
          %p
            As conceived by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structuralism"} structuralist
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            is the study of structures whose form is independent of the particular attributes of the things that make them up. For instance, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_N = \{ \emptyset, \{\emptyset\}, \{\emptyset, \{\emptyset\}\}, \dots \}
            or alternately as the Zermelo naturals
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi Z
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_Z = \{ \emptyset, \{\emptyset\}, \{\{\emptyset\}\}, \dots \}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} the category of sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#WhatNumbersCouldNotBe"} Benacerraf’s paper
          %p
            The structuralist says, essentially, that the number “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            ” should denote “the third place in a
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\emptyset, \{\emptyset\}, \{\emptyset, \{\emptyset\}\}\}
            as it does in any definition of “the set of natural numbers” in ZF.
          %p
            %em Structural set theory
            provides a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation+of+mathematics"} foundation of mathematics
            which is free of this “superfluous baggage” attendant on theories such as ZF, in which there is lots of information such as whether or not
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ∈
                  %mn 17
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3\in 17
            (yes, says von Neumann; no, says Zermelo) which is never used in mathematics. In a structural set theory, the elements (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %em have
            no identity apart from their existence as elements of that set, and whatever structure is given to that set by the functions and relations placed upon it. That is, sets (together with other attendant concepts such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theories"} material set theories
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em membership-based set theories
          %p
            Thus, somewhat paradoxically, it turns out that one of the primary attributes of a structural set theory is that the
            %em elements
            of a set have
            %em no
            “internal” structure; they are only given structure by means of functions and relations. In particular, they are not themselves sets, and by default cannot be elements of any other set (not in the sense that it is false that they are, but in the sense that it is meaningless to ask whether they are), so that elements of different sets cannot be compared (unless and until extra structure is imposed). Structural set theory thus looks very much like
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theories
            such as ZF, in which the elements of sets can have internal structure, and are often (perhaps always) themselves sets.
          %p
            Structural set theories can be distinguished by whether they include a formal notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family"} family
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+set+theory"} first-order set theories
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                zeroth-order set theories
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/zeroth-order+set+theory"} ?
            %span.newWikiWord
              Higher-order set theories
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/higher-order+set+theory"} ?
            have a notion of families of families in addition to families of sets. This parallels
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} propositions
            is played by sets and the role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicates
            is played by families. Many structural set theories are only zeroth-order set theories, but the hSets and functions from hSets to the type hSet in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            form a higher-order set theory.
          %p
            It is hard to say precisely what makes a set theory “structural”, but one attempt is the notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structurally+presented+set+theory"} structurally presented set theory
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#ETCS Elementary theory of the category of sets (ETCS)
          %p
            The original, and most commonly cited, categorially presented structural set theory is the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+theory+of+the+category+of+sets"} elementary theory of the category of sets
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            for short (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere65"}> Lawvere 65
            ).
          %p
            Therefore structural set theory is also called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong categorial set theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            axiomatizes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
            and thus lends itself to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"} h-set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#InHomotopyTypeTheory"}> below
            ), which forms a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CE%A0W-pretopos"} ΠW-pretopos
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            is weaker than
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+collection"} axiom of collection
          %p
            %a{:href => "#McLarty93"} McLarty 93
            argues that ETCS resolves the issues originally raised by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Benacerraf65"} Benacerraf 65
          %h3#sets_elements_and_relations_sear Sets, Elements and Relations (SEAR)
          %p
            Another structural set theory, which is stronger than ETCS (since it includes the axiom of collection by default) and also less closely tied to category theory, is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
          %h3#structural_zfc Structural ZFC
          %p
            One could reformulate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            as a three-sorted or dependently sorted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            consisting of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            axioms. This set theory is called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+ZFC"} structural ZFC
          %p
            It should also be possible to create an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/allegory"} allegorical
            version of ZFC, but nobody has done so yet.
          %h3#local_set_theory Local set theory
          %p
            Local set theory avoids the use of any global universe but instead is formulated in a many-sorted language that has various forms of sorts including, for each sort a power-sort; see
            %a{:href => "#Bell"} Bell
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Aczel"} Aczel
          %h3#setoids Setoids
          %p
            Set theory set up in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoids"} setoids
            is structural.
          %h3#InHomotopyTypeTheory Homotopy-sets in homotopy type theory
          %p
            The collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-sets"} h-sets
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            constitute a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CE%A0W-pretopos"} ΠW-pretopos
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#RijkeSpitter13"} Rijke-Spitters 13
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+all+propositions"} type of all propositions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-sets"} h-sets
            constitute an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
          %h2#related_pages Related Pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+versus+structural+set+theory"} material versus structural set theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structurally+presented+set+theory"} structurally presented set theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material-structural+adjunction"} material-structural adjunction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+set+theory"}> categorical set theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/allegorical+set+theory"} allegorical set theory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            A textbook that introduces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            informally via structural set theory is
          %ul
            %li#LawvereRosebrugh03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Rosebrugh"} Robert Rosebrugh
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets+for+Mathematics"} Sets for Mathematics
          %p
            The formal account of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            originates with
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere65
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An elementary theory of the category of sets
              %strong 52
              pp.1506-1511 (1965). (
              %a{:href => "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC300477/pdf/pnas00186-0196.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Structural set theory found in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is discussed in
